## Per-AC-A reasoning (independent verdict, not inherited from the plan)

The plan's four AC-A items all trace to the single in-scope PRD criterion,
AC-3. Each is walked on its own merits below.

**AC-A1 (shortcuts manifest member is well-formed).** `public/manifest.webmanifest`
is a static JSON asset with zero logic — Task 1 adds a literal object, not a
function. A Vitest test here could only assert that a JSON literal equals
itself once written (`shortcuts[0].url === "/new-task"`), which is exactly
the same content-invariant the plan's own Level 1/2 Validation Commands
already assert via a `node -e` script (plan lines 252-269, 336-353). This is
not a coverage gap: it is the identical treatment phase 1 gave the sibling
`share_target` member (`test-suite.diff` line 57: "a static JSON invariant on
a non-code asset, not application logic ... writing one would duplicate that
existing, plan-specified validation path"). Verdict: **NO_TEST_REQUIRED** —
covered by the plan's own non-Vitest content-invariant check, not a gap.

**AC-A2 (`main.tsx` detects `/new-task`, strips it via `history.replaceState`,
passes `null` `initialShare`).** This is DOM/browser code —
`window.location.pathname` and `window.history.replaceState` are browser
globals absent from workerd. `main.tsx` itself also calls
`document.getElementById("root")` and `createRoot(...)` at module scope, so
even importing the file under test would fail outside a real DOM. Unlike
Phase 1's `parseShareTarget`, this branch involves **no parsing, no
preference ordering, no transformation** — the plan is explicit that "no
parsing call is needed... `initialShare` stays at its default `null`" (plan
lines 277-280). There is no pure, extractable decision function hiding here:
the entire branch is `if (pathname === "/new-task") { replaceState(...) }`,
a one-line string comparison plus a side-effecting browser API call. The
plan's `## Files to Change` lists only `src/app/main.tsx` — no new
`src/shared` module — so inventing a pure `isNewTaskShortcut(pathname)`
helper to make this "testable" would mean authoring a test against an
implementation shape the plan never specified, which is exactly the
imagined-implementation anti-pattern (hard constraint 3) and would silently
force the Implementer into an unplanned refactor. The plan's own Level 2
grep check (`grep -q '"/new-task"' src/app/main.tsx`) and Level 3 dev-server
curl dry-run already exercise the observable route-resolution behavior with
a real exit code. Verdict: **NO_TEST_REQUIRED** — DOM-unreachable from the
only test tier, no pure unit exists in the plan's actual scope, and the
plan's own non-Vitest checks already cover what is left.

**AC-A3 (capture field opens focused and empty on `/new-task`).** This is
the PRD's own named example of a purely visual/gesture-based criterion
(`## TDD Routing`, "AC-3's focus behaviour"). The plan states explicitly
that "no functional change to `App.tsx` is required for AC-3 itself" (plan
lines 93-96) — the unconditional `autoFocus` and the
`initialShare?.title ?? ""` seeding are pre-existing, unmodified behavior
from Phase 1. There is nothing this phase changes to test, and the rendered
outcome (an input actually receiving DOM focus) is unreachable from workerd
regardless. Verdict: **NO_TEST_REQUIRED** — manual verification only (plan's
Level 3 MANUAL step 1), consistent with the PRD's explicit routing note.

**AC-A4 (the autofocus non-gating decision is recorded in code).** Task 3 is
a doc-comment-only edit — "the `autoFocus` attribute and every surrounding
line stay byte-identical" (plan lines 305-307). What AC-A4 actually requires
is that a comment string exists above a specific line; that is an
authorship/documentation invariant, not application behavior a running test
observes. The plan's own Level 2 Validation Commands already assert this
with a real exit code (`grep -q "Open Question 1" src/app/App.tsx`, plan
lines 313-319, 357-364). A Vitest test that reads `App.tsx`'s source text
and asserts a comment substring would not be testing the SUT's observable
behavior at all — it would be re-implementing the plan's own grep check in a
different runner, adding no discriminative power. Verdict:
**NO_TEST_REQUIRED** — covered by the plan's own non-Vitest content-invariant
check.

### Why this differs from phase 1

Phase 1's `parseShareTarget` was genuinely nontrivial: a preference order
across three fields, a documented Android quirk (`url` often empty, `text`
carries the link), and whitespace trimming — real decision logic with
multiple discriminable branches, living in `src/shared` (DOM-free, dual
environment-compiled) by the plan's own design, and 8 real cases were
written for it. Phase 3 has no analogous unit: every artifact this phase
touches is either static data (Task 1), a one-line already-covered DOM
branch with no `src/shared` counterpart (Task 2), or a comment with no
behavioral change (Task 3). This is not a weaker effort at finding a testable
seam; it is what an honest walk of this specific plan's three tasks turns up.

